Hey,

I have installed xenomai 2.5.5 using linux 2.6.31.8 and the corresponding
adeos patch adeos-ipipe-2.6.31.8-x86-2.4-09.patch.

I also tried to disable CONFIG_PCI_MSI by passing pci=nomsi in grub as
documented in /usr/src/linux/Documentation/kernel-parameters.txt. I did not
disable the CONFIG_PCI_MSI option since it is selected by: AMD_IOMMU &&
X86_64 && PCI && ACPI ...and I did not know which of these options I should
deselect. Also, when I disable ACPI completely, the kernel only detects a
single cpu, although there are 8 cores in the system.

I compiled xeno2.5.5 with and without can support (2 attached config files).

Without CAN drivers compiled (2.6.31.8-ipipe-xenomai_2.5.5), I managed to
boot the system 3 times in a row. However, when I also compiled the CAN
drivers (2.6.31.8-ipipe-xenomai_2.5.5-can) the system did not boot up
anymore.

Attached are the syslogs, which hopefully contain some useful debug
information.

- the non real time kernel (syslog_nonrt.txt)
- the real time that worked 3 times (syslog_rt_can_noMod.txt)
- the real time kernel with CAN drivers compiled in and which unfortunately
never booted (syslog_rt_can.txt)

I tried to avoid issues with the nvidia kernel module by not using the
nvidia kernel module but using the default "nv" driver. However, I am not
sure whether this made things better/worse or had any influence at all.
Interestingly, the assigned IRQ is 11 which is also used by the PEAK CAN-Bus
controller.

I have seen that there are many more kernel-parameters I could try out and
pass it using grub (I am running grub-pc ver. 1.97), but I am not sure
whether this could solve my problem.

I really appreciate any help and hope that the information provided is
useful.

THANKS so much,
 peter


